      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#1 · Nag Hammadi Codex II,4; Layton translation; Robinson's dating (before AD 350, attributed)",
          "title": "The arrogant ruler mistakes a local realm for the entirety",
          "italic": "The claim demonstrates the source-side operation: local power reads absence from its field as absence from reality, and claims total authority.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The tractate is a Gnostic exposition of the origin, nature and power of the rulers, its surviving Coptic text in Nag Hammadi Codex II. It begins from the rulers' claim: their chief is blind, powerful, ignorant, arrogant, and declares himself sole god. A voice from incorruptibility answers and names the error: he does not see the greater reality whose absence from his field he has mistaken for non-existence. The rebuke names him Samael; the cosmological",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "revelation gives Yaldabaoth and Sakla. Robinson's account places a Greek composition before AD 350 with Jewish or Jewish-Christian connections — a scholarly attribution; the heavens, abyss, garden and ark belong to narrated space. The developed telling follows the tractate's own disclosure: ruler and human formation first, Norea's crisis, then Eleleth's retrospective account of origins — the late revelation changing the reader's understanding of the earlier coercion.\n\nOccurrences: the sole-god declaration and the voice's rebuke (#1); the three names (#1, #3); the field's edge as the claim's condition (#1, #3).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
